  "response": "Adjudication between the two accounts begins with the magnitude that has to be explained. US headline CPI inflation reached 9.1 percent year over year in June 2022, and that reading confirms that inflation exceeded 8 percent year over year at some point during 2022. Either inflation breached 8 percent during that year or it remained below 8 percent year over year throughout 2022: one of the two must be the case, and they cannot both be. The analytical question is then which margin was binding, and at least one of two conditions must have obtained \u2014 nominal aggregate demand in the United States ran above its pre-pandemic trend during 2021, or global goods-producing capacity was constrained below pre-pandemic levels during 2021, and perhaps both held together.\n\nThe stimulus account rests on the scale of the policy impulse. The American Rescue Plan Act provided roughly $1.9 trillion in fiscal support, and it was precisely the size of that package that led Lawrence Summers, with the monetary expansion of 2020 already behind him, to warn in February 2021 that the fiscal package could ignite inflationary pressures. That fiscal injection in turn caused headline inflation to climb to the 9.1 percent peak recorded in June 2022, which is why the aggregate-demand reading of the episode begins with the legislation rather than with the price indices. The US M2 money stock grew by roughly 25 percent during 2020, and a monetary expansion on that scale shows that Shapiro's decomposition of the core basket, an exercise carried out at the San Francisco Fed, attributed essentially all elevated core inflation in 2022 to demand factors.\n\nPulling in the opposite direction, the New York Fed's Global Supply Chain Pressure Index hit a record high in December 2021, a reading constructed from shipping costs, delivery times and backlogs rather than from any measure of spending. The composition of price increases is examined in the same spirit, since a bottleneck account predicts that the goods most exposed to disrupted production should lead the acceleration. New and used vehicle prices surged in 2021 amid a semiconductor shortage, and that surge drove durable goods prices in the United States to rise faster than services prices during 2021.\n\nCross-country comparison is treated as the discriminating test, because a national fiscal and monetary impulse should not show up in economies that enacted nothing comparable, whereas a genuinely global shortage of goods and energy should raise prices nearly everywhere. The international record is accordingly the place where the two stories are forced apart. Euro area HICP inflation peaked at 10.6 percent in October 2022. Japan's consumer price inflation, measured on its own national index, peaked above 8 percent in 2022.\n\nWhat ultimately settles the question is the joint behaviour of quantities and prices rather than either series alone. If capacity constraints were doing the work, output in the affected sectors should have fallen while their prices rose, and the acceleration should have faded as backlogs cleared; if the spending impulse was decisive, both prices and quantities should have risen together, the acceleration should have spread from goods into rents, wages and other services, and persistence should have required monetary tightening to break. The observed sequence in the United States \u2014 early, concentrated goods inflation that subsequently broadened into services and nominal wages, and subsided only partially as freight rates and delivery times normalised \u2014 is why most practitioners now assign a substantial role to each mechanism, with disruption dominating the first phase and excess nominal demand accounting for the persistence that followed.",
